> "Heather Coppersmith" <me@privacy.net> wrote:
> 
> > Worse than that, gcc thinks that this is clear enough:
> > 
> >     if( (my_var = foo( )) )
> >         whatever( );
> 
> Well, it is, if you read C really well, since the extra
> parentheses around the assignment, whose value _is_ the
> value of the assignment, are an explicit prod to the C
> compiler to do the needed implicit cast to convert the
> value of the assignment to the compatible type of the
> just enclosing environment;

No, it isn't. Imprimis, there is no such thing as an implicit cast; a
cast is an _explicit_ request for conversion. What you meant is an
implicit _conversion_. And even implicit conversions do not rely on
extra parentheses; they occur exactly when an expression has a type
which both needs to and can be converted to the expected type, no matter
what this expression looks like.

> since that is the outer
> parentheses, which are expecting a value of type "bool",

No, they don't. Allow me to quote from the Standard:

# The controlling expression of an if statement shall have scalar type.

Yes, that's _any_ scalar type, even pointers.

> To me, though, that's still sloppy programming style,
> because the programmer has hidden information well within
> the programmer's ken, the (bool) cast and the "== true"
> comparision, which are useful to succeeding, perhaps less
> capable code maintainers.

There is no bool type in C unless you program in ISO C99, and NetHack
isn't even ISO C89. No (bool) cast would be needed in that context even
if there were. There is no true identifier in pre-1999 C. if (x) is true
for many more values that if (x==true), because if (x) triggers for
_all_ non-zero values - so if a real C programmer wants to make the test
explicit, he uses if (x==0) or if (x!=0). Yes, even for pointers.

And finally, if someone can't read perfectly ordinary, idiomatic C, he
has no bloody business putting his mangy hands on _my_ code.

